{:db/id 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068549 :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048943 :topic/math-academy-id 625 :topic/title "Finding the Existence of a Limit Using One-Sided Limits"} {:db/id 17592186051049 :topic/math-academy-id 1814 :topic/title "Infinite Limits from Graphs"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a2a5f0-3e42-57b4-bad9-206ee3780f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186438320} {:answer/id #uuid "af0d72cc-9b83-5e65-b119-ed68cb865633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186438321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59a2a5f0-3e42-57b4-bad9-206ee3780f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186438320} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf1a4d79-1b8d-5950-8a5e-bfcfed26db16" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186439189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61757e96-29e7-5ad6-b13f-368e70c8d57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186438322} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e26259-4957-5803-9056-827b1a4d22de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186438323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0e26259-4957-5803-9056-827b1a4d22de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186438323} :answer-field/id #uuid "2501ee59-3780-5be9-bc55-a5f8dde11fd5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186439190} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa1d56f-1dd9-5c6a-9405-26299564144c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186438324} {:answer/id #uuid "89ca0c5d-ca91-5313-8b14-509ad40774a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186438325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ca0c5d-ca91-5313-8b14-509ad40774a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186438325} :answer-field/id #uuid "59f15014-19b5-5e80-ad71-25e49dd00eea" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186439191}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "544abde9-c223-5348-b980-33df85581d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48042" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- **III.** $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id 17592186069367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1338885-9fa5-56a6-bbde-464a7beff78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186439807} {:answer/id #uuid "c73173bd-c83c-504e-9f9a-d56025e0fdde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186439808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73173bd-c83c-504e-9f9a-d56025e0fdde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186439808} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb748d5b-bb9b-5e8c-884e-a291b4ee024f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186440261} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9689ec4f-c734-5a92-8f3f-3bc8bc7f5697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186439809} {:answer/id #uuid "e4abe095-a9a5-582e-9ac5-95107ec344b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186439810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9689ec4f-c734-5a92-8f3f-3bc8bc7f5697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186439809} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e06b7f-98e6-5fb5-9ff3-c9f6a2f96bc6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186440262} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe5f199-131e-595e-9f80-3a37e7df7a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186439811} {:answer/id #uuid "da339f68-232e-501a-ad3b-79e19